---
import MainLayout from '../layouts/MainLayout.astro';
import ServiceEvidencePanel from '../components/ServiceEvidencePanel.astro';
import serviceEvidence from '../data/serviceEvidence.json';
import LegacyHeader from '../components/LegacyHeader.astro';
import Footer from '../components/Footer.astro';
import ContactSection from '../components/ContactSection.astro';
import Hero from '../components/Hero.astro';
import ButtonPrimary from '../components/ButtonPrimary.astro';
import ButtonSecondary from '../components/ButtonSecondary.astro';

const faqs = [
  {
    question: 'How is SaaS SEO different from other SEO?',
    answer: "A software buyer rarely searches once. They search for the problem, then for a type of product, then for comparisons, integrations and pricing, and they want to judge fit before they commit. Feature, use-case, integration and comparison pages each answer a different one of those questions, while documentation serves prospects and existing users alike. We set priorities around the journey your product actually offers: a trial, a demo or a self-serve purchase."
  },
  {
    question: 'What results have you achieved for SaaS and software companies?',
    answer: "Our published software case is TeamStats, a football team management app. Its Search Console screenshot shows 268k clicks for the last three months against 200k for the same period a year earlier, with average position moving from 11.4 to 9.1. These are comparison-period totals, not monthly traffic, signups or revenue. The linked case explains the work and the available evidence. We have also published reviews of AI referral reports for TeamStats and for a fintech software company."
  },
  {
    question: 'Does SEO make sense for SaaS when we already run paid acquisition?',
    answer: "It can, when buyers need product information your current pages do not give them. Paid campaigns buy the click; they do not answer the comparison, integration or use-case question the buyer arrived with. Compare paid and organic acquisition using your own signup or demo data. We look for missing comparisons, integration details and use cases before recommending more content."
  },
  {
    question: 'How important is AI search for SaaS companies?',
    answer: "It is worth evaluating alongside organic search, because software buyers do use assistants to draw up a shortlist. We define the questions, markets and platforms to monitor, check the accuracy of what the answers say about your product, and separate mentions from referral sessions and from verified signups or demos. Our two published software reviews show how we read an AI referral report and where its limits lie."
  },
  {
    question: 'What is included in a SaaS SEO campaign?',
    answer: 'A defined monthly scope across six workstreams: technical SEO (crawlability, site speed, schema, docs and app-subdomain handling), keyword and funnel research, on-page optimisation, content production or briefs for comparison and use-case pages, authority building through relevant links and digital PR angles, and AI search optimisation. Reporting ties the work to signups and demo requests where tracking allows, and says plainly where it does not.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you write comparison and alternative pages?',
    answer: "Yes, and we write them fairly. We check features, limits, pricing conditions and suitable use cases against current product information. Each comparison should help a buyer choose, including where another product is the better fit, because a one-sided page convinces nobody who has used both. Your product team reviews the factual claims before publication, and the page needs an owner who keeps it current."
  },
  {
    question: 'How long does SaaS SEO take to work?',
    answer: "Timing depends on the starting pages, the competition, product changes and your engineering capacity. We agree release milestones and reporting windows so that progress is judged on work shipped as well as on search data. Click growth in another software case does not set a deadline for your demos, activation or acquisition costs."
  },
  {
    question: 'How much does SaaS SEO cost?',
    answer: "We publish no rate card. We scope the work against your product, target customers and website, and the written proposal separates research, technical implementation, writing, product review and measurement requirements. A small set of comparison pages and a documentation migration are different jobs and need different budgets."
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you work with our product and engineering teams?',
    answer: "Yes. We agree which changes marketing can publish and which require engineering. Each technical recommendation identifies the affected URLs or templates, the proposed change and the acceptance checks, so it can go into your backlog as written. Delivery dates are planned around access and your release process."
  },
  {
    question: 'Do you understand product-led growth and freemium funnels?',
    answer: 'Yes. For a self-serve product, we review whether a visitor can understand the offer, start a trial and reach the first useful product action. Public docs, templates and tools may help that journey, but each needs a clear purpose and an owner who maintains it. We agree how to measure progress from the marketing site into the product, and we say where tracking is incomplete.'
  },
  {
    question: 'Can you handle international SEO for SaaS?',
    answer: "We can scope country and language targeting around the markets your product supports. We review regional availability, pricing, integrations and documentation before proposing hreflang or new market pages. Country-level traffic alone does not show that the product can serve those buyers, so the product facts come first."
  }
];
---

<MainLayout
  title="SaaS SEO Agency | Organic Growth for Software | Bright Forge"
  description="SaaS SEO for feature, comparison and integration pages, with technical review and reporting tied to signups where tracked. See our published TeamStats case."
  canonical="https://brightforge.com.ph/saas-seo-services/"
  type="service"
  faqs={faqs}
>
  <Fragment slot="head">
    <script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify({
      '@context': 'https://schema.org',
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://brightforge.com.ph/' },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'SaaS SEO Services', item: 'https://brightforge.com.ph/saas-seo-services/' }
      ]
    })} />
  </Fragment>

  <LegacyHeader slot="header" />

  <Hero
    badgeIcon="fas fa-rocket"
    badgeText="SaaS SEO Specialists"
    title="SaaS SEO Services for <span>Product Evaluation and Trial Journeys</span>"
    breadcrumbCurrent="SaaS SEO Services"
  >
    <Fragment slot="story">
      <p>People choosing software search for the problem, then for a type of product, then for comparisons and integrations. If your site answers only the first of those, the buyer finishes the evaluation on a competitor's pages. SaaS SEO builds the feature, comparison and integration pages for each stage and fixes the technical faults on the marketing site and public documentation that keep them from being found.</p>
      <p>Our published software work is with TeamStats, a football team management app: organic clicks rose from 200k to 268k over three months against the same period a year earlier. Those are search clicks, not signups, which is why our reporting plan separates search clicks, AI referrals and confirmed product actions.</p>
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="actions">
      <ButtonPrimary href="/contact/" text="Get a SaaS SEO Proposal" />
      <ButtonSecondary href="#saas-results" text="See SaaS Results" />
    </Fragment>

    <Fragment slot="visual">
      <div class="market-visual" aria-label="Published SaaS search evidence and AI referral reviews">
        <div class="market-node">
          <span>TeamStats</span>
          <strong>200k → 268k clicks, three-month year-on-year comparison</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="market-line"></div>
        <div class="market-node forge">
          <span>Fintech SaaS</span>
          <strong>AI referral report review: product facts, eligibility and integration details</strong>
        </div>
        <div class="market-line"></div>
        <div class="market-node">
          <span>Sports SaaS</span>
          <strong>AI referral report review: buyer questions and a route to try the product</strong>
        </div>
      </div>
    </Fragment>
  </Hero>

  <section class="proof-strip" aria-label="SaaS SEO proof points">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="proof-grid">
        <div><strong>200k → 268k clicks</strong><span>TeamStats, three-month year-on-year comparison</span></div>
        <div><strong>Two AI referral reviews</strong><span>Sessions, mentions and citations kept apart</span></div>
        <div><strong>Funnel-led content</strong><span>Comparison, use-case, docs</span></div>
        <div><strong>Engineering-friendly</strong><span>Specs your devs can ship</span></div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="who-for">
    <div class="container two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">Who this is for</p>
        <h2>A SaaS SEO agency for teams measured on <span>signups and demos, not sessions</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">A buyer comparing software wants more than a feature list. They want the supported workflows, the integration requirements, the plan limits and a way to test the fit. Where your site is silent, a review site or a rival's comparison page answers for you. We find which of those answers are missing before recommending a single new page.</p>
        <p>For self-serve products we examine the path into a trial and on to activation. For sales-led products we concentrate on what a buyer must know before a demo request is worth your sales team's time. The two journeys need different pages and different measurement.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="card-grid small">
        <article class="card"><h3>B2B SaaS</h3><p>Feature, use-case and comparison pages, plus content that reaches the people on the buying committee before your sales team does.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>PLG and freemium products</h3><p>SEO aimed at signups and activation, with docs, templates and free tools treated as acquisition pages that each need an owner.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Fintech and regulated software</h3><p>Pages where accuracy and visible sign-off matter as much as rankings, and every product claim is approved by your product and compliance teams.</p></article>
        <article class="card"><h3>Apps and platforms</h3><p>Consumer and prosumer apps competing for high-volume search demand, like our <a href="/case-studies/football-app-seo/">football app client</a>, TeamStats.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="pf-section" id="saas-start">
    <div class="pf-wrap">
      <p class="eyebrow">How to start</p>
      <h2>Three steps to <span>a scoped SaaS SEO proposal</span></h2>
      <p class="pf-lede">You do not need a brief. Send your product, target customers and trial or demo journey. We will identify the pages to review and the input needed from marketing, product and engineering.</p>
      <ol class="pf-steps">
        <li><strong>Send the product and the journey</strong><span>Your website, product documentation, the trial or demo path and the markets you serve.</span></li>
        <li><strong>We find the missing answers</strong><span>We come back with the missing feature, comparison and integration pages, the technical constraints and the product review the work would need.</span></li>
        <li><strong>You receive a written proposal</strong><span>It separates research, technical implementation, writing, product review and measurement, and says which changes marketing can publish and which need engineering.</span></li>
      </ol>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section darker" id="saas-results">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">Published SaaS results</p>
        <h2>Software search evidence <span>you can check for yourself</span></h2>
        <p>The TeamStats card is a Search Console comparison. The two AI-search cards are reviews of AI referral reports: they show what the reports recorded and what we would examine next, and they claim no verified pipeline or completed changes.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="results-grid">
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Sports App · Team Management</p>
          <h3>TeamStats: +34% comparison-period clicks</h3>
          <p>The screenshot shows 268k clicks for the last three months against 200k for the same period a year earlier, on impressions up from 7.14M to 9.23M. Average position moved from 11.4 to 9.1 across the site. The comparison does not establish individual rankings, signups or revenue.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/football-app-seo/">Read the case study →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Review focus · AI search</p>
          <h3>Fintech: make product limits clear</h3>
          <p>The report for an equity-management product covers August 2025 to January 2026, and the review sets out the AI referral sessions and key events it records. The events are not confirmed leads. A buyer here needs supported jurisdictions, reporting capabilities and integration requirements, so we identify the missing information for your product and compliance teams to review.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/ai-fintech-saas-geo/">Related AI-search review →</a>
        </article>
        <article class="result-card">
          <p class="result-tag">Review focus · AI search</p>
          <h3>Sports software: explain the product</h3>
          <p>The TeamStats report covers the same six months. The review sets out the AI referral sessions and key events it records, none of them confirmed sign-ups. A buyer of team-management software compares squad administration, fixtures and reporting, so we review whether those tasks have clear pages and a useful route into the product.</p>
          <a href="/case-studies/ai-sports-saas-geo/">Related AI-search review →</a>
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="services">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">What we deliver</p>
        <h2>Full-funnel <span>SEO for software companies</span></h2>
        <p>We choose among these workstreams according to the product journey and your release capacity. Each one has a named implementation owner and a review point before the next begins.</p>
      </div>

      <div class="service-grid">
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-filter"></i><h3>Funnel &amp; keyword research</h3><p>Problem, solution, comparison and pricing-stage demand, each mapped to the page type that should answer it. See <a href="/keyword-research-services/">keyword research</a>.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-balance-scale"></i><h3>Comparison &amp; alternative pages</h3><p>Pages that compare supported features, limits and fit honestly, reviewed against current product information and kept up to date as either product changes.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-cogs"></i><h3>Technical SEO</h3><p>Crawlability, rendering on JavaScript-heavy marketing sites, schema, docs and subdomain strategy via our <a href="/technical-seo-services-philippines/">technical SEO service</a>, delivered as specs your engineers can ship.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-pen-nib"></i><h3>Content production</h3><p>Use-case pages, integration pages and expert-led articles through our <a href="/content-seo-services-philippines/">content SEO service</a>, with factual review by your product specialists.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-link"></i><h3>Authority building</h3><p>Relevant <a href="/backlink-seo-services-philippines/">link building</a> and digital PR angles that make sense for a software brand. No bulk packages.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-robot"></i><h3>AI search optimisation</h3><p>Clear statements of what the product is and does, citable content and measured LLM visibility through our <a href="/ai-search-optimization-services-philippines/">AI search service</a>, with a defined monitoring sample.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-key"></i><h3>Competitor gap analysis</h3><p>We compare rival coverage with your supported features and your buyers' questions, then rank the missing pages by relevance and implementation effort.</p></article>
        <article class="service-card"><i class="fas fa-chart-line"></i><h3>Signup-led reporting</h3><p>Monthly reports tied to GA4: clicks, signups and demo requests where tracked, the pages that produced them, work completed and next priorities.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section darker" id="ai-search">
    <div class="container two-col">
      <div>
        <p class="eyebrow">The AI search shift</p>
        <h2>Make product answers <span>clear enough to check</span></h2>
        <p class="lead">An assistant asked to compare software repeats whatever it can find. A buyer comparing equity-management software, for example, may need to know which jurisdictions and reporting workflows a product supports. Those answers need product approval before they become comparison copy or material for AI-search monitoring.</p>
        <p>When we monitor answers we record the question, the platform and the observation date. If an answer misstates a feature or an eligibility rule, we check the source page before recommending a correction. A mention is reported separately from a visit, and a visit separately from a product action.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="timezone-card">
        <h3>What we track for AI visibility</h3>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>LLM referral sessions</strong>: identifiable sessions referred by AI platforms</li>
          <li><strong>Completed actions</strong>: verify the event and destination before counting an enquiry</li>
          <li><strong>Mentions and citations</strong>: where AI platforms name and link your product</li>
          <li><strong>Platform gaps</strong>: where answers omit or misstate product information in the agreed sample</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section dark" id="process">
    <div class="container">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">How a campaign runs</p>
        <h2>A delivery sequence for <span>your SaaS SEO campaign</span></h2>
      </div>

      <div class="timeline">
        <article><span>Diagnose</span><h3>Funnel diagnosis</h3><p>A technical audit of the marketing site and the indexable parts of the app, keyword mapping by buying stage, competitor gaps and a priority plan tied to signups as well as traffic.</p></article>
        <article><span>Implement</span><h3>Foundations</h3><p>Technical fixes shipped with your engineers, on-page work on the commercial pages, and the comparison-page plan agreed with your product team.</p></article>
        <article><span>Publish</span><h3>Content delivery</h3><p>Use-case, comparison and integration pages go into production, highest value first. Authority building starts on the terms those pages target.</p></article>
        <article><span>Review</span><h3>Review and prioritise</h3><p>We review clicks, signups and AI visibility against the plan, then put more effort where the data supports it and stop what it does not.</p></article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="section faq-section">
    <div class="container narrow">
      <div class="section-head centered">
        <p class="eyebrow">SaaS SEO FAQs</p>
        <h2><span>Questions software teams ask</span> before working with us</h2>
      </div>

      <div class="faq-list">
        {faqs.map((faq) => (
          <details class="faq-item">
            <summary>{faq.question}</summary>
            <p>{faq.answer}</p>
          </details>
        ))}
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="cta-section">
    <div class="container narrow cta-box">
      <p class="eyebrow">Ready to talk?</p>
      <h2>Get a straight answer about <span>SEO for your product</span></h2>
      <p>Send your website, product documentation and trial or demo path, along with the markets you serve. We will come back with the missing pages, the technical constraints and the review work a proposal would need to cover, usually within one business day.</p>
      <div class="cta-actions">
        <a href="/contact/" class="cta-primary">Get a SaaS SEO Proposal</a>
        <a href="/case-studies/" class="cta-secondary">View All Case Studies</a>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <ServiceEvidencePanel panel={serviceEvidence['saas-seo-services']} />

  <ContactSection slot="contact" />
  <Footer slot="footer" />
</MainLayout>

<style>
.container {
  width: min(1180px, calc(100% - 2rem));
  margin: 0 auto;
}

.narrow {
  width: min(900px, calc(100% - 2rem));
}

.market-visual {
  position: relative;
  display: grid;
  gap: 1.1rem;
  max-width: 480px;
  margin: 0 auto;
  padding: 1.5rem;
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 107, 53, 0.25);
  border-radius: 28px;
  background: linear-gradient(145deg, rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.09), rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.09));
  box-shadow: 0 25px 80px rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.09);
}

.market-node {
  padding: 1.2rem;
  border-radius: 18px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.06);
  border: 1px solid rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.09);
}

.market-node.forge {
  border-color: rgba(255, 107, 53, 0.35);
}

.market-node span {
  display: block;
  color: #b83e12;
  font-size: 0.82rem;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  margin-bottom: 0.35rem;
}

.market-node strong {
  display: block;
  color: #19212e;
  font-size: 1.05rem;
}

.market-line {
  height: 26px;
  width: 2px;
  background: linear-gradient(#ff6b35, transparent);
  margin-left: 1.2rem;
}

.proof-strip {
  background: #ffffff;
  border-top: 1px solid rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.08);
  border-bottom: 1px solid rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.08);
  padding: 1.5rem 0;
}

.proof-grid {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
  gap: 1rem;
}

.proof-grid div {
  padding: 1.1rem;
  border-radius: 16px;
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.045);
  border: 1px solid rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.08);
}

.proof-grid strong,
.proof-grid span {
  display: block;
}

.proof-grid strong {
  color: #19212e;
  margin-bottom: 0.25rem;
}

.proof-grid span {
  color: #465365;
  font-size: 0.92rem;
}

.section {
  padding: 5.5rem 0;
}

.dark {
  background: #f7f8fa;
}

.darker {
  background: #ffffff;
}

.two-col {
  display: grid;
  grid-template-columns: minmax(0, 0.9fr) minmax(0, 1.1fr);
  gap: 3rem;
  align-items: center;
}

.eyebrow {
  color: #b83e12;
  font-weight: 700;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.1em;
  font-size: 0.82rem;
  margin-bottom: 0.85rem;
}

h2 {
  color: #19212e;
  font-size: clamp(2rem, 4vw, 3.2rem);
  line-height: 1.05;
  margin-bottom: 1.25rem;
}

h3 {
  color: #19212e;
  font-size: 1.2rem;
  margin-bottom: 0.75rem;
}

p,
li {
  color: #465365;
  line-height: 1.75;
}

.lead {
  font-size: 1.15rem;
  color: #465365;
}

.section-head {
  max-width: 820px;
  margin-bottom: 3rem;
}

.centered {
  text-align: center;
  margin-left: auto;
  margin-right: auto;
}

.card-grid,
.service-grid,
.results-grid,
.timeline {
  display: grid;
  gap: 1.25rem;
}

.card-grid.small {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
}

.card,
.service-card,
.result-card,
.timezone-card,
.timeline article,
.faq-item {
  background: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.055);
  border: 1px solid rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.09);
  border-radius: 22px;
  padding: 1.5rem;
}

.card a,
.service-card a {
  color: #b83e12;
}

.service-grid {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
}

.service-card i {
  display: inline-grid;
  place-items: center;
  width: 46px;
  height: 46px;
  margin-bottom: 1rem;
  border-radius: 14px;
  color: #19212e;
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, #ff6b35, #ff9f1c);
}

.results-grid {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(3, 1fr);
}

.result-tag {
  color: #b83e12;
  font-size: 0.82rem;
  text-transform: uppercase;
  letter-spacing: 0.08em;
  margin-bottom: 0.5rem;
}

.result-card a {
  display: inline-block;
  margin-top: 0.75rem;
  color: #b83e12;
  font-weight: 700;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.timeline {
  grid-template-columns: repeat(4, 1fr);
}

.timeline span {
  display: inline-block;
  color: #b83e12;
  font-weight: 800;
  margin-bottom: 0.9rem;
}

.timezone-card {
  background: linear-gradient(145deg, rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.08), rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.035));
}

.timezone-card ul {
  list-style: none;
  padding: 0;
  margin: 1.25rem 0 0;
}

.timezone-card li {
  position: relative;
  padding-left: 1.75rem;
  margin-bottom: 0.8rem;
}

.timezone-card li::before {
  content: '✓';
  position: absolute;
  left: 0;
  color: #b83e12;
  font-weight: 800;
}

.faq-section {
  background: #f7f8fa;
}

.faq-list {
  display: grid;
  gap: 1rem;
}

.faq-item summary {
  cursor: pointer;
  color: #19212e;
  font-weight: 700;
  font-size: 1.08rem;
}

.faq-item p {
  margin-top: 1rem;
}

.cta-section {
  padding: 5rem 0;
  background: #ffffff;
}

.cta-box {
  text-align: center;
  padding: 3rem;
  border-radius: 28px;
  background: linear-gradient(135deg, rgba(255, 107, 53, 0.18), rgba(255, 159, 28, 0.08));
  border: 1px solid rgba(255, 107, 53, 0.28);
}

.cta-actions {
  display: flex;
  justify-content: center;
  flex-wrap: wrap;
  gap: 1rem;
  margin-top: 2rem;
}

.cta-primary,
.cta-secondary {
  display: inline-flex;
  align-items: center;
  justify-content: center;
  min-height: 48px;
  padding: 0.85rem 1.4rem;
  border-radius: 999px;
  font-weight: 800;
  text-decoration: none;
}

.cta-primary {
  color: #0d0f1a;
  background: #fff;
}

.cta-secondary {
  color: #19212e;
  border: 1px solid rgba(25, 33, 46, 0.09);
}

@media (max-width: 980px) {
  .proof-grid,
  .service-grid,
  .timeline {
    grid-template-columns: repeat(2, 1fr);
  }

  .results-grid {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }

  .two-col {
    grid-template-columns: 1fr;
  }
}

